*list: expandable
	*classes: FAQ-box
	See full rules text (click to expand)
		*html
			<div class="full-rules-box">
			<h1>Unanswered Questions in Psychology Prize</h1>
			<h3>Full Rules and Guidelines</h3>
			
			<p>Inspired by the Millennium Problems in mathematics, the Unanswered Questions in Psychology Prize aims to identify the most important open questions in psychology.</p>
		
			<p>We are seeking submissions of questions that, in the view of academic psychologists, represent especially important unresolved issues in the field of psychology.</p>
		
			<p>Winning submissions will be selected based on their perceived importance, as judged by academic psychologists through a voting process. The Clearer Research team will be responsible for de-duplicating similar entries, and reducing the set of submissions to a manageable number for voting.</p>
		
			<h2>1. Eligibility</h2>
		
			<p>Anyone may submit any number of questions. We anticipate most questions will be submitted by academic psychologists, but that is not a requirement for entry.</p>
			
			<p>Submissions may be made by anyone whatsoever, without limitation. However, voting on the winners will be limited only to academic psychologists (current or former). The exact definition of an academic psychologist used for the purposes of this prize will be determined by the Clearer Research team.</p>
			
			<p>There are no geographic restrictions on participation.</p>
			
			<p>To be eligible for the prize (including monetary award), submitters must provide their full name and a valid email address. Prizes will be paid via PayPal (or, when that is not possible, via wire transfer).</p>
			
			<p>Members of the Clearer Research team may submit questions but will not be eligible to vote on winning submissions (only current and former academic psychologists with no affiliation to Clearer Research will be permitted to vote). Submissions from the Clearer Research team are also not eligible for prize money.</p>
			
			<h2>2. What to Submit</h2>
			<p>Submissions must include:<br>
			● One "Unanswered Questions in Psychology" question per submission (multiple submissions per person are allowed, but they must be submitted one at a time).<br>
			● Answers to two demographic questions: one about the submitter's relevant fields of expertise, the other about the submitter's level of education. These questions are included for research purposes only and answers given to these questions will not be used (in any way) in the process of selecting winners.</p>
			
			<p>Required for prize eligibility:<br>
			● The name of the submitter<br>
			● A valid, working email address at which to contact the submitter<br>
			Submissions without a name or email address are permitted but authors of such submissions will not be eligible to receive the prize and monetary award (even if their question is selected as a winner).</p>
			
			<p>Submissions may include (at the submitters discretion):<br>
			● An explanation of the question's importance. This may be especially useful for questions the importance of which is not immediately obvious, such as those that are particularly technically specialized.</p>
			
			<p>Any submitter choosing to give their name will be asked whether they are willing to grant permissions for the Clearer Research team to (a) publish the submitter's question, explanation, and name in the event that their question is selected as a winner, and to (b) use the submitter's question, explanation, and name in communication about and promotion of the prize, prior to the selection of winners.</p>
			
			<h2>3. Multiple Submissions</h2>
			<p>There is no limit on:<br>
			● the number of submissions any individual is permitted to submit<br>
			● the number of submissions submitted by any individual that may be selected for the voting stage</p>
			
			<p>Any individual seeking to submit multiple questions must submit each one separately.</p>
			
			<p>In the event that multiple questions submitted by the same individual are selected as winners of the prize, that individual will receive a number of prizes and associated monetary awards equal to the number of winning questions that individual submitted.</p>
			
			<h2>4. Scope of Acceptable Questions</h2>
		
			<p>Questions should pertain to the subject matter of psychology, broadly construed.</p>
			
			<p>Questions should not pertain to meta-level questions about psychology as a discipline. For example, questions such as "Should we get rid of p-values?", or "How do we increase interdisciplinary collaboration?" are beyond the scope of this prize because they are meta-level questions about the field rather than questions about psychology itself. This exclusion does not reflect a judgment about their importance.</p>
			
			<p>Questions may draw on or intersect with adjacent fields such as neuroscience, philosophy, economics, or public policy. However, submissions must remain substantively about psychological phenomena.</p>
		
			<p>Questions may be very broad (e.g., "What determines long-term subjective well-being?"), very narrow, or anywhere between.</p>
		
			<p>Questions may be empirical, theoretical, or conceptual.</p>
		
			<p>Questions are not required to be currently answerable, or even answerable in principle.</p>
			
			<p>The Clearer Research team may reword submissions for clarity and to make formatting/structure uniform across entries.</p>

		
			<h2>5. Evaluation Criteria</h2>
		
			<p>The Clearer Research team will determine which questions to "longlist" for voting. Those longlisted will then be voted on by academic psychologists, and it is this vote that will determine the final winners. While the Clearer Research team will decide on the longlist, and the longlist must be at least twice as many submissions as the total number of prizes planned. Members of the Clearer Research team are not permitted to vote on the winning submissions.</p>
		
			<p>Submissions will be evaluated first and foremost on importance. Ultimately, it is up to voters to decide what 'important' means to them, though we do intend to give suggestions, which may include factors like:<br>
			● the potential of the question to significantly advance understanding<br>
			● its relevance to real-world outcomes or to alleviating societal challenges<br>
			● its capacity to shape future research directions<br>
			● its foundational or unifying role within psychology</p>
		
			<p>Final judgments are left to the discretion of voters and the Clearer Research team.</p>
		
			<h2>6. Number of Winners and Prizes</h2>
			
			<p>There is no fixed number of winners. The exact number of winning entries will be determined by the Clearer Research team, and will be at minimum 8 (as long as at least 40 submissions are received from academic psychologists not affiliated with Clearer Research - if fewer than 40 submissions are received from academic psychologists not affiliated with Clearer Research, the prize may be canceled, with no prizes paid out).</p>
			
			<p>Each eligible winning submission will receive a prize of $400 USD.</p>
			
			<p>Both winning and non-winning submissions may be listed on our website (e.g., as "top mentions", "runners up", etc., or simply being listed as a full set of submissions). By making a submission you grant us permission to use that submission for any purpose whatsoever, including listing it on our website and newsletter.</p>
			
			<p>Members of the Clearer Research team may make submissions but cannot be awarded prize money if such a submission were to be selected by voters as a top submission (i.e., the money would be awarded to the next highest rated submission from a non-team member, so that such submissions cannot impact the prize money awarded to external submitters).</p>
		
			<h2>7. Judging Process</h2>
		
			<p>Winners will be determined through a voting process by academic psychologists. Since there is no fixed number of winners, the Clearer Research team will determine the cut-off and number of winners after voting has concluded.</p>
		
			<p>The Clearer Research team will administer and oversee the process.</p>
			
			<h2>8. Duplicate and Similar Submissions</h2>
		
			<p>We expect that multiple participants will submit similar or identical questions.</p>
		
			<p>In such cases:<br>
			● A maximum of one version of the question will be voted upon<br>
			● This will typically be the earliest submitted version that is phrased with adequate clarity (only the first to submit the idea with adequate clarity will be eligible for the prize), but may involve merging two or more submissions in cases where questions are not merely duplicates but are on the same topic and additive to each other.<br>
			● When explanation text is required to convey the importance of the question, the quality of explanation text will be considered as a factor in which submission is selected to be voted upon</p>
		
			<p>Other similar submissions will not be considered separately.</p>
		
			<h2>9. Submission Editing and Resubmission</h2>
		
			<p>Submissions cannot be edited by submitters after they are submitted. However, participants may submit the same underlying idea again as a new submission, for example:<br>
			● to improve wording<br>
			● to clarify the question<br>
			● to provide a better explanation</p>

			<p>Each submission will be treated independently.</p>
		
			<h2>10. Use of Submissions and Privacy</h2>
			
			<p>As part of the submission process, submitters may choose whether to provide their full name, title, and email address, or to remain anonymous.</p>
			
			<p>By submitting to the prize, submitters grant the Clearer Research team permission to publish and share the question and explanation text associated with their submission. Once winners have been selected, the Clearer Research team may publish or share the question, explanation text, and (where provided) the name and title provided in any and all submissions.</p>
			
			<p>Submitters who choose to provide their name will be asked whether they grant permission for their name (and title, when given) to be published or shared alongside their question and explanation text in promotional material for the prize, prior to winners being selected. The Clearer Research team may publish or share such information in accordance with the permissions given by the submitter. Submitters who do not choose to submit their name will not be asked the additional permission question and the Clearer Research team reserves the right to publish the question and explanation given in any such submission without attribution. A name and email must be submitted in order for a submission to be eligible to receive prize money.</p>
			
			<p>Any data provided that is not a submitter's question, explanation, or name will never be published or shared externally except:<br>
			1. in aggregated and anonymized form as part of data analyses or reporting about the prize (for example, "X number of people with Y specialization submitted questions").<br>
			2. some third-party service providers (such as Google Analytics) may process or temporarily store data as part of providing services to us. They do not have permission to use or disclose this data for any purpose other than to render their services to us.</p>
		
			<p>E.U. Citizens: Please note that your data will be stored outside of the European Union, including potentially in the U.S. and in other countries where we may have servers or databases.</p>
		
		
			<h2>11. AI-Generated Submissions</h2>
		
			<p>AI assistance is permitted, but submissions should primarily reflect the submitter's own thinking.</p>
			
			<p>We strongly discourage the use of AI-generated submission and explanation text. If AI is used in the process, we recommend rewording submission to be in your own words.</p>
			
			<p>Submissions may be rejected at the discretion of the Clearer Research team (e.g., if they appear to be low quality, or plagiarized).</p>
			
			<h2>12. Timeline</h2>
		
			<p>Submission deadline: November 10th, 2026</p>
			<p>Voting and winner announcement timelines will be communicated to participants after submissions close.</p>
			
			<h2>13. Final Authority</h2>
		
			<p>All decisions regarding:<br>
			● eligibility<br>
			● interpretation of rules<br>
			● duplicate submissions<br>
			● AI-related concerns<br>
			● and prize awards<br>
			are made at the discretion of the Clearer Research team, whose decisions are final.</p>
			</div>


